  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nGray skies persist for yet another day as two broad surface\nhighs, one to our southeast and one well to our northwest, have\nforced a narrow, southwest- to northeast- oriented moisture \naxis from the Deep South through the Midwest and into our \nregion. Simultaneous to this moisture advection, warm air has\nalso been advecting into our region ahead of a low-level warm\nfront, driving seasonably mild temperatures in the upper 60s to\nlow 70s. Weak reflectivities have also been noted on the KENX \nradar throughout the day as a result. However, ample low- level\ndry air courtesy of the nearby high has, per latest obs,\nsignificantly limited the amount of rain that has reached the \nground.\n\nLight sprinkles or virga will persist, mainly west of the Hudson\nRiver Valley, through this evening before more widespread \nchances of rain increase after midnight with the approach of a \ncold front. An area of showers will then spread into the region\nfrom northwest to southeast, likely breaking up as it tracks \nfarther south and east, especially south and east of the Capital\nDistrict, due to the confrontation with persistent low-level dry\nair. Therefore, due to the swift nature of the front and a \ngeneral lack of potent forcing, overall rainfall amounts are \nanticipated to be light. The greatest amounts are expected \nacross the Southern Adirondacks where the steadiest rain is \nlikely. Here, QPF ranges from ~0.25\" to 0.5\" while a couple \nhundredths to about 0.15\" is expected elsewhere. \n\nSaturday will finally be the day that we consistently see the\nsun as subsidence behind the departing front increases with the\nbuilding of an anticyclone from the west. While temperatures\ntomorrow will remain abnormally warm given the warmer than\nnormal airmass overhead (850 hPa temperatures ~1.5 to 1.8 \nSTDEVs above normal), the passage of the front will cool things\nback towards normal for Saturday and Sunday. That said, heading\ninto early next week, a closed low well to our north will extend\na deepening trough into our region. The northwest flow regime\nthat this trough will create will usher in a cooler than normal\nairmass that could be cause for some frost formation in areas\nwhere the growing season remains ongoing.",
